Building Space Observatories Above Earth's Atmosphere: A Theoretical Framework for Scalable Orbital Astronomy

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI : chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek 

Date: 2026

Abstract

This dissertation establishes a rigorous mathematical foundation for the design and optimisation of next-generation space observatories, conceived not as isolated monolithic telescopes but as distributed, adaptive orbital sensing networks. A unified multi-objective optimisation framework is developed that balances scientific information yield, angular resolution, system cost, and energy expenditure under orbital and communication constraints. The core of the framework is a state-space model of observatory dynamics coupled with Bayesian optimal experimental design, permitting the formal derivation of information-theoretic performance bounds.

The thesis provides complete proofs of optimal orbital configurations for continuous sky coverage, derives scaling laws that delineate regimes in which distributed architectures surpass single-aperture systems, and develops computational reconstruction algorithms that reduce hardware complexity through distributed interferometry and compressed sensing. An end-to-end technical specification of a four-layer architecture—orbital infrastructure, sensor network, computational layer, and scientific output—is presented with quantitative parameters. The work contributes a generalised information geometry for multi-messenger astronomy, a set of optimal control policies for adaptive scheduling, and a systems-engineering blueprint for a scalable, evolvable orbital observatory ecosystem.

