## Chapter 7: Proposed Architecture and Technical Specification

### 7.1 Layer 1: Orbital Infrastructure

**LEO Constellation:** 24 satellites in a Walker-Star configuration ($24/6/0$): 6 equally-spaced orbital planes, 4 satellites per plane, altitude 600 km, inclination $97.6°$ (sun-synchronous to maintain consistent thermal environment and solar panel illumination). The Walker-Star topology ensures complete sky coverage with maximum coverage gap $< 15$ minutes and revisit time $< 5$ minutes for $> 80$% of the sky. Ground-track spacing in right ascension of ascending node: $\Delta\Omega = 30°$. Satellite spacing within each plane: $90°$.

**L2 Formation:** 4 spacecraft at Sun-Earth L2 in a halo orbit ($A_y \approx 800{,}000$ km Lissajous or JWST-class halo orbit), deployed in a tetrahedral formation with projected baselines 50–500 km. The tetrahedral geometry simultaneously maximises $(u,v)$-coverage in three orthogonal projections while maintaining passive thermal stability ($\Delta T < 1$ K over an orbit). Station-keeping $\Delta v$ budget: $< 5$ m s$^{-1}$ per year.

**Relay and Communications Architecture:** LEO nodes transmit to the nearest ground station (Ka-band, 1 Gbps per link) and to L2 nodes via optical inter-satellite links (ISL, 1 Gbps). L2 nodes downlink via X/Ka-band at 500 Mbps to a 34 m DSN-class station.

**Inclination Correction from Standard Walker-Delta.** The LEO constellation uses a Walker-Star (polar) topology, *not* a Walker-Delta (inclined). The Walker-Star is optimal for sky coverage because it provides equal access time to all celestial latitudes and avoids the under-coverage of polar ecliptic regions that a Walker-Delta would produce.

### 7.2 Layer 2: Sensor Network

Each node carries an optical telescope of either Cassegrain or Three-Mirror Anastigmat (TMA) off-axis design:

**LEO Nodes:** Primary aperture 0.5–1.0 m (diameter), f/12 to f/15 effective focal ratio. Wavelength range 0.2–2.5 µm (UV through H-band NIR). Detector complement:

- UV/optical (200–900 nm): Back-illuminated silicon CMOS sensor, 4k × 4k pixels, 10 µm pixel pitch, read noise < 3 $e^-$ rms, dark current $< 0.01$ $e^-$ s$^{-1}$ pixel$^{-1}$ at $-100°$C.
- NIR (0.9–2.5 µm): HgCdTe (HAWAII-4RG class) array, 4k × 4k pixels, cutoff wavelength 2.5 µm, detector temperature 85 K (passive cooling feasible at 600 km LEO with a multi-layer radiator).

Laser metrology: optical path difference (OPD) measurement to 1 nm rms via heterodyne laser ranging (1319 nm or 1064 nm reference laser), enabling coherent beam combination if nodes achieve $< \lambda/20$ OPD matching.

Integral field spectrograph (IFS): $R \approx 100$–3000 resolution, covering 0.3–1.8 µm, 50 × 50 spaxel field of view.

**L2 Nodes:** Primary aperture 2.0–2.5 m, TMA off-axis. Detector complement as above plus mid-infrared capability (3–5 µm HgCdTe or GeSb focal plane at 35 K, achieved via Joule-Thomson or pulse-tube cryo-cooler). Laser metrology: picometer-class OPD control for nulling interferometry.

**Coronagraphic capability on L2 nodes:** An internal coronagraph (vector vortex or apodised pupil Lyot) with inner working angle $\sim 3\lambda/D$ enables direct imaging of exoplanets at contrasts of $10^{-9}$–$10^{-10}$ for L2 configurations with active wavefront control.

### 7.3 Layer 3: Computational Layer

**On-board Processing:** Each node hosts an FPGA-based real-time pipeline for cosmic ray rejection (using temporal differencing at ~5 s frame cadence), wavelet compression (compression ratio 10:1 lossless region, 50:1 science-acceptable), and local Bayesian posterior update (Kalman filter variant for the information-form update). Total on-board compute: ~100 GFLOPS (FPGA) + 10 TFLOPS peak (space-grade GPU for image reconstruction during downlink gaps).

**Inter-Satellite Links:** Optical (free-space laser) ISLs at 1550 nm, 1 Gbps sustained, range up to 2000 km for LEO-to-LEO and up to 1.8 million km for LEO-to-L2 (relay via intermediate node). Link budget includes 3 dB pointing margin and 6 dB atmospheric scintillation margin for LEO.

**Distributed Inference Engine:** The consensus algorithm (Theorem 6.3) runs on a sub-graph of 4 neighbouring nodes at each epoch, exchanging compressed sufficient statistics ($\sim 10^4$ floats per update). Full network convergence to $< 1$% relative error in the global posterior covariance requires $< 30$ consensus rounds for a 28-node star-topology network (theoretical bound from $\lambda_2$ of the star graph Laplacian: $\lambda_2 = 1$, giving linear convergence per round).

**Adaptive Scheduler:** A distributed scheduler running the greedy EIG policy (4.13) accesses a shared priority catalogue via gossip broadcast, with scheduling decisions at 60 s granularity.

### 7.4 Layer 4: Scientific Output

**Deep-Field Survey:** Coordinated 1000 h exposure with all 24 LEO nodes, effective area $\sim 50$ m² (combined), reaching AB magnitude 30 ($10\sigma$) at 500 nm. Survey speed (sky area per unit time at $10\sigma$, 500 nm) is $\sim 20\times$ faster than JWST for equal depth, because parallel sky coverage compensates for smaller per-node aperture.

**Exoplanet Science:** Transit survey of bright ($V < 11$) nearby stars with multi-node, multi-wavelength light curves enabling atmospheric characterisation via transmission spectroscopy (500–1800 nm simultaneously). Direct imaging via nulling interferometry on L2 four-node formation: baseline 50–200 km, inner working angle at 10 µm $\sim 0.01$ arcsec (sufficient for Jupiter-analogue detection around nearby stars). For habitable-zone Earth analogues, direct coronagraphic imaging by the 2.5 m L2 nodes achieves contrast $10^{-10}$ at $\sim 3 \lambda/D$, complementing the HWO mission scope with a distributed, multiwavelength perspective.

**Cosmological Measurements:** Coordinated weak gravitational lensing survey over $\sim 10{,}000$ deg² with sub-milli-arcsecond PSF stability (enabled by diffraction-limited space imaging). Supernova Ia monitoring cadence: any point in the Northern/Southern continuous viewing zones accessible with $< 1$ h latency.

**Multi-messenger follow-up:** Network is pre-programmed with automatic response protocols for GW counterpart searches: the full LEO constellation can retile the GW 90% credible region (typically 100–1000 deg²) within a single orbit (~90 min) at multiple wavelengths simultaneously.

