A.1 Ground Track Density for a Single Orbital Plane (Lemma 3.1)

We consider a circular orbit of radius R with inclination i. The satellite’s position in Earth-centred inertial (ECI) coordinates can be expressed using the argument of latitude u = \omega + \nu (where \omega is argument of periapsis, \nu true anomaly; for a circular orbit \nu is the mean anomaly, and we can set \omega=0 without loss of generality). The geocentric latitude \phi (spherical latitude) of the sub‑satellite point is given by

\sin\phi = \sin i\,\sin u .
\tag{A.1}

Because the orbit is circular, the angular velocity is constant, so u is uniformly distributed over [0,2\pi) in time. The probability density of u is therefore p(u) = 1/(2\pi) for u\in[0,2\pi).

To obtain the density \rho(\phi) of the sub‑satellite latitude, we use the transformation of random variables:

\rho(\phi) = \frac{1}{2\pi}\left|\frac{du}{d\phi}\right|.
\tag{A.2}

From (A.1), differentiate implicitly:

\cos\phi\,\frac{d\phi}{du} = \sin i\,\cos u .

Hence

\frac{du}{d\phi} = \frac{\cos\phi}{\sin i\,\cos u}.

Using \cos u = \sqrt{1-\sin^2 u} and \sin u = \sin\phi/\sin i from (A.1), we obtain

\cos u = \sqrt{1 - \frac{\sin^2\phi}{\sin^2 i}} = \frac{\sqrt{\sin^2 i - \sin^2\phi}}{|\sin i|}.

Thus

\left|\frac{du}{d\phi}\right| = \frac{\cos\phi}{\sin i} \cdot \frac{|\sin i|}{\sqrt{\sin^2 i - \sin^2\phi}} = \frac{\cos\phi}{\sqrt{\cos^2\phi - \cos^2 i}},

where we used \sin^2 i - \sin^2\phi = \cos^2\phi - \cos^2 i. The absolute value is absorbed because we take positive root. Consequently,

\rho(\phi) = \frac{1}{2\pi} \frac{\cos\phi}{\sqrt{\cos^2\phi - \cos^2 i}}, \qquad |\phi| \le i,
\tag{A.3}

and \rho(\phi)=0 for |\phi|>i. Up to a normalisation constant, this is the expression given in Lemma 3.1. For a polar orbit i=90^\circ, \cos i =0 and the density becomes \rho(\phi)\propto 1/\cos\phi, which diverges at the poles—confirming the concentration of sub‑satellite points at high latitudes.

---

A.2 Spherical Covering and the Minimum Satellite Bound

A.2.1 Proof of Theorem 3.1 (No Exclusion Zone)

Each satellite has a conical field of regard (FoR) of half‑angle \gamma, which projects onto the celestial sphere as a spherical cap of solid angle

\Omega_{\text{cap}} = 2\pi(1-\cos\gamma).
\tag{A.4}

The total solid angle of the sphere is 4\pi. A necessary condition for complete coverage (every direction covered at least once) is

N \cdot \Omega_{\text{cap}} \ge 4\pi.

Hence

N \ge \frac{4\pi}{2\pi(1-\cos\gamma)} = \frac{2}{1-\cos\gamma}.
\tag{A.5}

For small \gamma, use \cos\gamma \approx 1 - \gamma^2/2, giving N \ge 4/\gamma^2. This lower bound can be approached asymptotically by a hexagonal arrangement of caps on the sphere (the Tammes problem solution). The covering density of equal circles on a sphere is minimised by the hexagonal packing; the excess factor over the area bound is approximately the covering density factor for a plane, which is \approx 1.209 for large N. Hence the bound (A.5) is tight up to a constant.

A.2.2 Proof of Theorem 3.2 (With Earth‑Limb Exclusion)

When an Earth‑limb exclusion angle \epsilon>0 is imposed, directions at elevation below \epsilon as seen from the satellite are inaccessible. This effectively reduces the usable FoR half‑angle from \gamma to \gamma_{\text{eff}} = \gamma - \epsilon (assuming \gamma > \epsilon; if \gamma \le \epsilon, no sky is accessible). Replacing \gamma by \gamma-\epsilon in (A.5) yields

N_{\min} \ge \frac{2}{1-\cos(\gamma-\epsilon)} \approx \frac{4}{(\gamma-\epsilon)^2},
\tag{A.6}

which is larger than the bound without exclusion, as physically expected. The previously seen erroneous form with a plus sign would predict a smaller required number, contradicting the fact that exclusion shrinks the accessible cap.

