A.3 Multi‑Shell Coverage (Proposition 3.1)

Let \bar{\eta}_{\text{LEO}}(\hat{\mathbf{s}}) and \bar{\eta}_{\text{L2}}(\hat{\mathbf{s}}) be the time‑averaged probabilities that a given sky direction \hat{\mathbf{s}} is within the instantaneous FoR of at least one node in the LEO and L2 shells, respectively. Assuming independence of coverage events between the two layers (justified because their orbital dynamics and pointing constraints are decoupled), the probability that the direction is not covered by the combined network is the product of the probabilities that it is not covered by either layer:

1 - \bar{\eta}_{\text{combined}}(\hat{\mathbf{s}}) = \bigl(1 - \bar{\eta}_{\text{LEO}}(\hat{\mathbf{s}})\bigr)\bigl(1 - \bar{\eta}_{\text{L2}}(\hat{\mathbf{s}})\bigr).

Rearranging gives (3.7). With the numerical values given in the main text, the combined coverage fraction exceeds 0.95 for nearly all sky directions.

