A.4 Relative Motion at L2: Hill–Clohessy–Wiltshire Equations

Consider a reference circular halo orbit (or large Lissajous orbit) about the Sun–Earth L2 point. In the circular restricted three‑body problem, linearised relative motion in the rotating frame centred at the reference point is described by the Hill–Clohessy–Wiltshire (HCW) equations, which also apply to a chief satellite and a deputy satellite formation. In the co‑rotating frame with axes (x,y,z) (x radial, y along‑track, z out‑of‑plane), the linearised equations for the deputy’s relative position \delta\mathbf{r} = (x,y,z)^T are

\begin{aligned}
\ddot{x} - 2n\dot{y} - 3n^2 x &= a_x^{\text{ctrl}},\\
\ddot{y} + 2n\dot{x} &= a_y^{\text{ctrl}},\\
\ddot{z} + n^2 z &= a_z^{\text{ctrl}},
\end{aligned}
\tag{A.7}

where n = \sqrt{\mu/a^3} is the mean motion of the primary orbit (Sun–Earth system around their barycentre; for L2 halo orbits an equivalent mean motion can be used in the linearised framework), and a^{\text{ctrl}} are control accelerations. Writing in matrix form,

\begin{pmatrix} \ddot{x} \\ \ddot{y} \\ \ddot{z} \end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} 3n^2 x + 2n\dot{y} \\ -2n\dot{x} \\ -n^2 z \end{pmatrix} + \mathbf{u}_{\text{rel}},
\tag{A.8}

which is equation (3.6) after setting control inputs \mathbf{u}_{\text{rel}}. These equations admit bounded periodic solutions (passively safe relative orbits) when the initial conditions satisfy the in‑plane drift condition \dot{y}_0 + 2n x_0 = 0. Such trajectories are exploited for interferometric (u,v) plane filling without continuous thrust.

