Appendix B: Information‑Theoretic Derivations

---

B.1 Poisson Fisher Information and the Bayesian Cramér–Rao Bound

Fisher information for a Poisson count. Let d \sim \text{Poisson}(\mu(\theta)), with \mu(\theta)>0 differentiable. The log‑likelihood is \log p(d|\theta) = d\log\mu(\theta) - \mu(\theta) - \log d!. The score is

s(\theta) = \frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}\log p(d|\theta) = \frac{d}{\mu(\theta)}\mu'(\theta) - \mu'(\theta) = \frac{\mu'(\theta)}{\mu(\theta)}(d - \mu(\theta)).

The Fisher information is the variance of the score:

\mathcal{I}_1(\theta) = \operatorname{Var}\bigl[s(\theta)\bigr] = \left(\frac{\mu'(\theta)}{\mu(\theta)}\right)^2 \operatorname{Var}(d) = \frac{[\mu'(\theta)]^2}{\mu(\theta)},
\tag{B.1}

because \operatorname{Var}(d)=\mu(\theta). This is equation (4.1).

Bayesian Cramér–Rao bound (van Trees inequality). Let p(\theta) be a prior density satisfying \int (\partial\log p/\partial\theta)^2 p(\theta)\,d\theta < \infty. Define the prior Fisher information \mathcal{I}_{\text{prior}} = \mathbb{E}_\theta[-\partial^2\log p(\theta)/\partial\theta^2]. For any estimator \hat{\theta}(D) with finite second moment, the Bayesian mean squared error satisfies

\mathbb{E}\bigl[(\hat{\theta} - \theta)^2\bigr] \ge \frac{1}{\mathcal{I}_N + \mathcal{I}_{\text{prior}}},
\tag{B.2}

where \mathcal{I}_N is the (expected) Fisher information from the data, \mathcal{I}_N = \mathbb{E}_\theta[\mathcal{I}_{\text{data}}(\theta)]. A sketch of the proof: consider the combined random variable (\theta,D). The joint Fisher information for \theta is \mathcal{I}_{\text{joint}} = \mathcal{I}_{\text{prior}} + \mathbb{E}_\theta[\mathcal{I}_{\text{data}}(\theta)]; the classic Cramér–Rao bound for the unconditional expectation yields (B.2). For independent identical observations, \mathcal{I}_{\text{data}}(\theta)=N\mathcal{I}_1(\theta), giving the form in the text.

---

B.2 Mutual Information and the Gaussian Approximation

The expected information gain (EIG) is the mutual information between parameters \boldsymbol{\Theta} and data D:

I(\boldsymbol{\Theta};D) = \iint p(d,\boldsymbol{\theta}) \log\frac{p(\boldsymbol{\theta}|d)}{p(\boldsymbol{\theta})}\,d\boldsymbol{\theta}\,dd = h(\boldsymbol{\Theta}) - h(\boldsymbol{\Theta}|D),

where h denotes differential entropy.

Assume a Gaussian prior \boldsymbol{\Theta} \sim \mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\text{prior}}, \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\text{prior}}) and a linear(ised) Gaussian likelihood, leading to a Gaussian posterior \boldsymbol{\Theta}|D \sim \mathcal{N}(\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{\text{post}}, \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\text{post}}). The differential entropy of a p-dimensional Gaussian with covariance \boldsymbol{\Sigma} is

h = \frac{1}{2}\log\det(2\pi e\,\boldsymbol{\Sigma}) = \frac{p}{2}(1+\log 2\pi) + \frac{1}{2}\log\det\boldsymbol{\Sigma}.

Thus

I = \frac{1}{2}\log\det\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\text{prior}} - \frac{1}{2}\log\det\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\text{post}}.

Using the information form \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\text{post}}^{-1} = \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\text{prior}}^{-1} + \mathcal{I}_{\text{total}}, we have \det\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\text{post}} = 1/\det(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\text{prior}}^{-1} + \mathcal{I}_{\text{total}}). Then

I = \frac{1}{2}\log\det\bigl[\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\text{prior}}(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\text{prior}}^{-1} + \mathcal{I}_{\text{total}})\bigr] = \frac{1}{2}\log\det(\mathbf{I} + \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\text{prior}}\mathcal{I}_{\text{total}}),
\tag{B.3}

which matches the corrected formula. This expression is invariant under similarity transformations and clearly exhibits the dependence on the prior‑weighted Fisher information.

---

B.3 Proof of Information Scaling (Theorem 4.1) and Concavity (Theorem 4.2)

Assume \mathcal{I}_{\text{total}} = N\mathcal{I}_1 and let \lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_p \ge 0 be the eigenvalues of \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\text{prior}}^{1/2}\mathcal{I}_1\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\text{prior}}^{1/2} (which are the same as those of \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\text{prior}}\mathcal{I}_1). Then

I(N) = \frac{1}{2}\sum_{k=1}^p \log(1 + N\lambda_k).

For large N and any fixed \lambda_k>0,

\log(1 + N\lambda_k) = \log N + \log\lambda_k + O(N^{-1}).

Summing over the p non‑zero eigenvalues (the zero eigenvalues contribute zero) yields

I(N) = \frac{p}{2}\log N + \frac{1}{2}\sum_{k=1}^p \log\lambda_k + O(N^{-1}) = \frac{p}{2}\log N + \frac{1}{2}\log\det(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\text{prior}}\mathcal{I}_1) + O(N^{-1}),

provided all \lambda_k>0. This proves Theorem 4.1.

Differentiating twice with respect to N:

\frac{dI}{dN} = \frac{1}{2}\sum_{k=1}^p \frac{\lambda_k}{1+N\lambda_k},\qquad
\frac{d^2 I}{dN^2} = -\frac{1}{2}\sum_{k=1}^p \frac{\lambda_k^2}{(1+N\lambda_k)^2} < 0,

since at least one \lambda_k>0. Hence I(N) is strictly concave. This diminishing‑returns property justifies the existence of a finite economically optimal network size.

---

B.4 Submodularity and the Greedy Scheduling Guarantee

The one‑step expected information gain \Delta I(\hat{\mathbf{s}}, \mathbf{x}) when pointing a node at direction \hat{\mathbf{s}} is a submodular set function with respect to the set of selected observations, under mild conditions. The key idea: the mutual information I(\boldsymbol{\Theta};\{d_j\}) as a function of the set of observations is submodular because adding an observation to a larger set of previous observations yields a smaller marginal gain. Formally, for a sequence of observation decisions \mathcal{A}_t, the function F(\mathcal{A}) = I(\boldsymbol{\Theta};D_{\mathcal{A}}) is submodular and non‑decreasing.

The greedy policy that selects at each step the observation maximising the marginal gain (the “myopic” EIG maximisation) achieves at least (1-1/e) of the optimal value of the sequential decision problem over a finite horizon, provided the problem can be cast as maximising a submodular function under a cardinality constraint (Nemhauser–Wolsey–Fisher theorem). The time‑discounted infinite‑horizon problem (4.12) can be approximated by a finite‑horizon rollout; the myopic policy retains the same approximation ratio for submodular rewards when the dynamics are deterministic or the policy evaluation is consistent.

---

B.5 Fisher Information for Interferometric Imaging (Proposition 4.1)

For a complex visibility measurement V(u,v) = \mathcal{V}(u,v) + \epsilon, where \epsilon \sim \mathcal{CN}(0,\sigma_V^2) (circularly symmetric complex Gaussian noise), the log‑likelihood for the true visibility is

\log p(V|\mathcal{V}) = -\frac{|V - \mathcal{V}|^2}{\sigma_V^2} + \text{const}.

The Fisher information matrix for the image pixel vector \mathbf{I} (real‑valued) is

[\mathcal{I}_{\text{image}}]_{kl} = -\mathbb{E}\!\left[\frac{\partial^2 \log p}{\partial I_k \partial I_l}\right].

From the measurement equation \mathcal{V}(u,v) = \sum_{k} I_k e^{-2\pi i(u\ell_k + v m_k)} (discrete representation, complex amplitude), the derivative is \partial \mathcal{V}/\partial I_k = e^{-2\pi i(u\ell_k + v m_k)}. For a single (u,v) point, the contribution to the Fisher matrix (per real parameter) is \frac{1}{\sigma_V^2}\mathbf{a}\mathbf{a}^H where \mathbf{a} is the steering vector. Summing over all measured (u,v)\in\mathcal{U} gives

\mathcal{I}_{\text{image}} = \frac{1}{\sigma_V^2} \sum_{(u,v)\in\mathcal{U}} \mathbf{a}(u,v)\mathbf{a}^H(u,v),

which yields (4.9) when writing the product explicitly. Uniform coverage of the (u,v) disc maximises the determinant of this matrix (D‑optimal design), because the Fourier vectors become approximately orthogonal, making \mathcal{I}_{\text{image}} proportional to the identity.

