Appendix C: Network Optimisation Proofs

---

C.1 Resolution–Information Trade‑Off (Theorem 5.1)

Photon‑noise‑limited regime: For a point source, the photon count from a single aperture of area a is Poisson with mean \mu \propto a. The Fisher information per node is \mathcal{I}_i \propto a. With N identical nodes, \mathcal{I}_{\text{total}} \propto N a = A_{\text{tot}}, independent of how A_{\text{tot}} is divided.

Read‑noise‑limited regime: In a read‑noise dominated measurement, the signal is still proportional to a, but the additive Gaussian read noise \sigma_r per pixel does not scale with a. The Fisher information for the amplitude scales as (\partial S/\partial I)^2 / \sigma_r^2 \propto a^2. Summing over N nodes gives \mathcal{I}_{\text{total}}^{\text{RN}} \propto N a^2 = A_{\text{tot}}^2/N. Thus splitting total area into more sub‑apertures reduces the total Fisher information in the read‑noise regime. The resolution metric R = \log(L\,\theta_{\text{ref}}/\lambda) depends only on the formation size L and not on the diameter d of the individual apertures (provided L \gg d). This proves the theorem.

---

C.2 Homogeneous Network Optimality (Theorem 5.2)

We maximise the Lagrangian objective \mathcal{L} = \sum_{i=1}^N \log(1 + A_i/\sigma^2) - \mu c_1 \sum_i A_i^\kappa - \mu c_2 N subject to \sum_i A_i = A_{\text{tot}}. The first‑order condition for node i is

\frac{1}{\sigma^2}\frac{1}{1 + A_i/\sigma^2} - \mu c_1 \kappa A_i^{\kappa-1} = \lambda,

where \lambda is the multiplier for the area constraint. Because the left‑hand side depends only on A_i and is identical for all nodes, symmetry implies A_i = A_{\text{tot}}/N for all i. Strict concavity of g(A_i) = \log(1+A_i/\sigma^2) - \mu c_1 A_i^\kappa (for \kappa>1) guarantees that this symmetric point is the unique global maximum. The KKT conditions are thus satisfied, confirming that a homogeneous network is cost‑optimal under the given model.

---

C.3 Crossover Condition (Theorem 5.3)

Set the total collecting area equal: A_{\text{dist}} = N a = A_{\text{mono}}. Under photon‑noise‑limited conditions, the scientific information I is identical for both architectures. The resolution advantage of the distributed array is \Delta R = \log(L/D_{\text{mono}}). The cost of the monolithic telescope is C_{\text{mono}} = c_1 A_{\text{mono}}^\kappa + c_2. The cost of the distributed array is

C_{\text{dist}} = c_1 N \left(\frac{A_{\text{mono}}}{N}\right)^\kappa + c_2 N = c_1 A_{\text{mono}}^\kappa N^{1-\kappa} + c_2 N.

The energy difference \Delta E accounts for formation‑keeping. The composite objective difference is

\Delta J = \beta \Delta R - \gamma (C_{\text{dist}} - C_{\text{mono}}) - \delta \Delta E.

The distributed array is superior when \Delta J > 0, i.e.,

\beta \log\!\left(\frac{L}{D_{\text{mono}}}\right) > \gamma \bigl[ c_1 A_{\text{mono}}^\kappa (N^{1-\kappa} - 1) + c_2(N-1) \bigr] + \delta \Delta E.

Because N^{1-\kappa} < 1 for \kappa>1, the cost term can be negative (the distributed array may be cheaper), making the inequality easier to satisfy. This is exactly condition (5.1).

---

C.4 LQR Formation‑Keeping and Energy Scaling

Linearised relative dynamics at L2 are given by (A.8). A linear quadratic regulator (LQR) minimising the cost functional J = \int (\mathbf{x}^T\mathbf{Q}\mathbf{x} + \mathbf{u}^T\mathbf{R}\mathbf{u})\,dt with state \mathbf{x} = (\delta\mathbf{r}, \delta\dot{\mathbf{r}})^T and control \mathbf{u} = \mathbf{a}^{\text{ctrl}} yields a stationary feedback gain. For a desired formation of size L (separation), the perturbation forces are dominated by solar radiation pressure (SRP) differential \sigma_{\text{SRP}} \propto L (for two satellites with identical properties but slightly different attitudes) and tidal gradients. The disturbance power scales with L^2. The control effort (propellant mass) to maintain a given OPD stability scales as the integral of the control variance. Solving the LQR problem gives an average \Delta v per satellite approximately proportional to L^2\, T, leading to an energy cost E_{\text{FK}} \propto L^2. The exact proportionality constants depend on the thruster specific impulse and control allocation, but the quadratic scaling in baseline L is the key result.

