Appendix D: Computational Optics Proofs

---

D.1 Phase Retrieval Uniqueness (Theorem 6.1)

A real, non‑negative, compactly supported brightness distribution I(\boldsymbol{\ell}) has a Fourier transform \hat{I}(\mathbf{u}) that extends to an entire function of two complex variables (Paley–Wiener theorem). Suppose two distinct distributions I_1,I_2 yield the same Fourier modulus |\hat{I}_1(\mathbf{u})| = |\hat{I}_2(\mathbf{u})| on an open set \mathcal{U}\subset\mathbb{R}^2. Then on \mathcal{U} we have \hat{I}_1(\mathbf{u}) = e^{i\phi(\mathbf{u})}\hat{I}_2(\mathbf{u}) for some real phase \phi(\mathbf{u}). Because \hat{I}_1,\hat{I}_2 are entire, the function \hat{I}_1/\hat{I}_2 is meromorphic; its logarithmic derivative shows that \phi(\mathbf{u}) must be analytic wherever \hat{I}_2\neq0. On the open set where it is analytic, the real phase function can only be an affine function \phi(\mathbf{u}) = c_0 + \mathbf{c}_1\cdot\mathbf{u} (by considering the Cauchy–Riemann equations for \log \hat{I}_1 - \log \hat{I}_2). This corresponds to an overall translation of the image and a possible sign change (due to Hermitian symmetry of the Fourier transform of a real function). Non‑negativity and compact support eliminate the sign ambiguity, leaving only translation and a 180° rotation as degrees of freedom. This is the uniqueness result. Iterative algorithms (Fienup) converge to the correct solution for almost all practical scenes; convex lifting approaches (PhaseLift) provide guarantees under certain random sampling conditions.

---

D.2 Compressed Sensing Error Bound (Theorem 6.2)

The standard compressed sensing theorem (Candès, Romberg, Tao) states: if the sensing matrix \boldsymbol{\Phi} satisfies the Restricted Isometry Property (RIP) of order 2K with constant \delta_{2K} < \sqrt{2}-1, then for any vector \mathbf{x}, the solution \hat{\mathbf{x}} to the basis pursuit denoising problem

\min_{\mathbf{z}} \|\mathbf{z}\|_1 \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \|\mathbf{y} - \boldsymbol{\Phi}\mathbf{z}\|_2 \le \epsilon

satisfies

\|\hat{\mathbf{x}} - \mathbf{x}\|_2 \le C_0 \frac{\|\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{x}_K\|_1}{\sqrt{K}} + C_1 \epsilon,

where \mathbf{x}_K is the best K-sparse approximation. This is directly applied with \boldsymbol{\Phi} being the partial Fourier measurement matrix and \boldsymbol{\Psi} a sparsity basis (so the effective sensing matrix is \boldsymbol{\Phi}\boldsymbol{\Psi}). The constants C_0,C_1 depend only on \delta_{2K}.

RIP for orbital (u,v) samples: A partial Fourier matrix with randomly selected frequencies satisfies the RIP with high probability when the number of measurements M \ge C K \log^4 L. Although orbital dynamics produce deterministic, quasi‑random (u,v) tracks, the non‑coplanar geometry introduces a w-term that acts as a spread‑spectrum modulation, breaking the coherence between the Fourier measurement vectors and making the sensing matrix behave similarly to a random one. Simulations with typical orbital geometries show stable image reconstruction from severely undersampled data, supporting the practical validity of the RIP assumption.

---

D.3 Convergence of Distributed Consensus (Theorem 6.3)

The synchronous linear consensus update for the information matrix \mathbf{J}_i^{(t)} (vectorised) is

\mathbf{J}_i^{(t+1)} = \mathbf{J}_i^{(t)} - \alpha \sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}_i} (\mathbf{J}_i^{(t)} - \mathbf{J}_j^{(t)}),

which, in concatenated vector form over all nodes, becomes \mathbf{J}^{(t+1)} = (\mathbf{I} - \alpha \mathcal{L}) \mathbf{J}^{(t)}, where \mathcal{L} is the Laplacian matrix of the communication graph. The matrix \mathbf{W} = \mathbf{I} - \alpha\mathcal{L} has eigenvalues \mu_k = 1 - \alpha\lambda_k, where \lambda_k are the eigenvalues of \mathcal{L} (0 = \lambda_1 < \lambda_2 \le \cdots \le \lambda_N). The eigenvector \mathbf{1} with eigenvalue 1 corresponds to the consensus subspace (all nodes equal). All other eigenvalues satisfy |\mu_k| < 1 if 0 < \alpha < 2/\lambda_N. The slowest contraction rate is determined by the second largest eigenvalue in magnitude, which for optimal \alpha is \max\{|1-\alpha\lambda_2|, |1-\alpha\lambda_N|\}. With optimal \alpha = 2/(\lambda_2 + \lambda_N), this rate is (\kappa-1)/(\kappa+1) where \kappa = \lambda_N/\lambda_2. Hence the deviation from the average decays as O(e^{-t/\tau}) with time constant \tau = 1/\log(1/\rho); equivalently, the disagreement reduces geometrically at rate \rho^t. This proves exponential convergence.

---

D.4 Super‑Resolution Localisation (Theorem 6.4)

For a two‑element interferometer, the phase of the complex visibility is \phi = 2\pi B\sin\theta/\lambda \approx 2\pi B\theta/\lambda for small \theta. The measurement of the visibility under thermal noise provides an effective observation d = A e^{i\phi} + n with n \sim \mathcal{CN}(0,\sigma^2). The Fisher information for \theta is

\mathcal{I}(\theta) = \frac{2}{\sigma^2}\left|\frac{\partial (A e^{i\phi})}{\partial \theta}\right|^2 = \frac{2A^2}{\sigma^2}\left(\frac{2\pi B}{\lambda}\right)^2.

If we collect N_{\text{phot}} photons in total, the complex visibility amplitude A is proportional to \sqrt{N_{\text{phot}}} and the noise variance scales as 1/N_{\text{phot}}. More precisely, the achievable signal‑to‑noise ratio on the fringe is \text{SNR} = V \sqrt{N_{\text{phot}}}, where V is the fringe visibility \eta. The resulting Fisher information is \mathcal{I}(\theta) = (2\pi B/\lambda)^2 N_{\text{phot}} V^2. Including independent measurements on both real and imaginary parts yields the same scaling; the Cramér–Rao lower bound on the variance is \sigma_\theta^2 \ge 1/\mathcal{I}(\theta) = \lambda^2/(4\pi^2 B^2 N_{\text{phot}} \eta). This yields the stated uncertainty.

---

D.5 RIP and Spread‑Spectrum from the w-Term

For a non‑coplanar array, the visibility equation includes a w-component:

\mathcal{V}(u,v,w) = \iint \frac{I(\ell,m)}{\sqrt{1-\ell^2-m^2}} e^{-2\pi i (u\ell + vm + w(\sqrt{1-\ell^2-m^2}-1))}\,d\ell\,dm.

The exponential factor e^{-2\pi i w(\sqrt{1-\ell^2-m^2}-1)} acts as a spatially varying phase modulation that depends on the (time‑varying) w coordinate. This modulation scrambles the Fourier encoding, breaking the regular Fourier structure and making the measurement matrix “incoherent” with respect to sparse bases like wavelets. The effect is analogous to that of the spread‑spectrum sampling in compressive imaging, and it improves the effective RIP constant when the w values vary sufficiently over the observations. This property underpins the favourable compressed sensing recovery observed in orbital interferometry.

---

Appendix E: Sensitivity and Performance Limits

---

E.1 Limiting Flux Derivation (Equation 8.1)

For a point source with flux F (photons per unit area per unit time), the signal collected in integration time \Delta t is S = \eta A F \Delta t, where \eta is the total throughput and A the collecting area. The noise variance has three components: photon shot noise from the source, background (zodiacal light), and dark current. In the background‑limited regime, the dominant noise is the zodiacal background. The background count within the photometric aperture (assumed to match the PSF solid angle \Omega_{\text{PSF}}) is B_{\text{sky}} \Omega_{\text{PSF}} \Delta t in units of photons, where B_{\text{sky}} is the background surface brightness (photons per unit time per unit solid angle per unit area). The total noise rms is

\sigma = \sqrt{\eta A B_{\text{sky}} \Omega_{\text{PSF}} \Delta t + N_{\text{dark}}\Delta t + \text{other terms}}.

For a N_\sigma detection, the required flux satisfies S = N_\sigma \sigma. Solving for F:

F_{\min} = \frac{N_\sigma \sqrt{\eta A B_{\text{sky}} \Omega_{\text{PSF}} \Delta t + N_{\text{dark}}\Delta t}}{\eta A \Delta t}.

Neglecting dark current for background‑limited observations, and noting \Omega_{\text{PSF}} \approx (\lambda/D)^2, this gives (8.1). Converting to AB magnitude uses m_{\text{AB}} = -2.5\log_{10}(F/\text{zeropoint}). With the L2 zodiacal background and \eta=0.3, the derived sensitivity matches JWST‑like performance.

---

E.2 OPD Control and Coherence Length

For interference of light of centre wavelength \lambda and bandwidth \Delta\lambda, the temporal coherence length is l_c = \lambda^2/\Delta\lambda. To maintain high fringe visibility, the optical path difference (OPD) between the two arms must be kept constant to within a fraction of l_c (typically \ll l_c/20). For a narrowband fringe‑tracking channel with \Delta\lambda=1 nm at \lambda=500 nm, l_c = 250\,\mu\text{m}, requiring an OPD stability of <5 nm rms over the integration time. Laser metrology (heterodyne interferometer) with picometre resolution at kHz bandwidth can achieve this, as demonstrated in laboratory tests and planned for missions like SILVIA.

---

E.3 Survey Speed Scaling

The survey speed (area covered to a given depth per unit time) for a distributed network is proportional to the number of independent pointings, assuming each node can observe a different field. The total etendue (or “AΩ product”) sums across nodes. For a fixed total collecting area A_{\text{tot}}, distributing it among many smaller telescopes reduces the per‑pointing sensitivity but allows many parallel exposures. The survey speed scales as N \times (area of sky per exposure), and the per‑exposure time for a given depth scales as 1/A_i. For N identical apertures of area A_i = A_{\text{tot}}/N, the survey speed for a fixed depth is proportional to N \cdot (A_i)^{-1} \sim N^2 if we ignore background‑limited regime complications. In practice, the gain is limited by the background noise floor and readout overheads, but factors of 20–100 over a single equivalent aperture are achievable, as stated in Chapter 8.

